n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(4\\sqrt{2},{135}^{∘}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545799 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545799 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545799 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545799 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545799 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/59/59ad56e7eae8e8598ebd511a7b499f35cd6a178405f8198831b23e8d5d47a513.png)\n\nThe circles $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $  intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545799 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d83a7e0-a78a-57e3-b959-267d62524fd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545799 #edb/ref 1074 "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n \n\n$$\n(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545804 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545804 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545804 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545804 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545804 #edb/ref 1069 "q-325647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545804 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545804 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/82/823ff7d93c17556b8587da62430c3168bbd6d49da43e88450f0804d83598f23f.png)\n\nThe circles $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=1$ intersect for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in radians.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$),\\,$\n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(${{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545804 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2156ef1c-7aad-5ba1-895f-5a7013935e5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545804 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nAlso, notice that $r=1$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(1,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545810 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324188" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545810 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545810 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/40/40db66aea0bf6de657ea7fa673ece434c01fa81ae0f7c3664cf9bf1cf8fa02e0.png)\n\nThe polar curves $r=5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect  for $\\theta \\in [{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘})$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in degrees.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$\n$\\,and\\,$ \n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(${{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}${}^{∘})$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545810 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d565dfb-3434-5111-8bf3-8db207613208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545810 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={30}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={150}^{∘}.$ \n\nNow, we use the equation $r=1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{1}=1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 \\\\ {r}_{2} & =1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{2}=1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(3,{30}^{∘})\\,\\text{and }({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(3,{150}^{∘}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545816 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545816 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545816 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f5/f556adb5f1af77ed89db28638a19051b6ebb8ff056eabe60a8a6357984011a12.png)\n\nThe circles $r=1$ and $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $B?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545816 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47f83011-f0ac-5fe7-b577-1abb06b9a992" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545816 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={60}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={300}^{∘}.$  \n\nAlso, notice that $r=1$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $B$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(1,{300}^{∘}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545833 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545833 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545833 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f7/f78441133b6c3ac196e9e9e8dcfbbbdef875468e54ee34a3509b26d7ed86578e.png)\n\nThe circles $r=2$ and $r=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $B?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545833 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d90f0eb-b4e6-5cf1-abd5-55559dbeabf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545833 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={45}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={135}^{∘}.$ \n\nAlso, notice that $r=2$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $B$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(2,{135}^{∘}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545836 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545836 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545836 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545836 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b8/b80f9b57f7ed0e79a9ebbe387d5cedc1a46e23da2d498599bc48ee52c76ff190.png)\n\nFind the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves  $r=2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=1,$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92072883-4631-52cf-ad3b-532b194d125c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545836 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,r & =2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\,r & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={30}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={150}^{∘}.$  \n\nNotice that we must have $r=1$ for any point that lies on both curves. \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(1,{30}^{∘})\\,$ and $\\,(r,\\theta )=(1,{150}^{∘}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545844 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545844 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545844 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545844 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545844 #edb/ref 1071 "The curves $r=2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ intersect at a single point in the third quadrant. Determine the polar coordinates of this point. Express the polar angle $\\theta $ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545844 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a30f6995-a7bb-57e9-9de6-99d42c4d49a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545844 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2 & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the $\\theta $-coordinate of the point that we're interested in is $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ since it lies in the third quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of our point:\n\n$$\nr=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the desired intersection point are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{7}{2},\\text{ }\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545847 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545847 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121800" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545847 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545847 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9e/9e7a57e93473508aa49868e75769d905f52be9b3b6c74bfd9328b5e9bfe240c7.png)\n\nThe circles $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545847 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5a55304-1327-53a7-ab60-6725a01ed1fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545847 #edb/ref 1074 "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection.\n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =-\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta ={315}^{∘}$ in the $4$th quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545851 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545851 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121848" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545851 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545851 #edb/ref 1071 "The curves $r=2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ intersect at a single point in the second quadrant. Determine the polar coordinates of this point." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545851 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b30c011d-e3fe-5a6f-9c15-6828aaa923bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545851 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the $\\theta $-coordinate of the point that we're interested in is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ since it lies in the second quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of our point:\n\n$$\nr=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the desired intersection point are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545859 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ce/ce263ef057316e67e65b3adf1a5d66797c6a97c9bdcd33cae866d9111e523e10.png)\n\nThe curves $r=\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in radians.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$),\\,$\n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c975a336-1c2a-5428-a315-180711c9bbb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545859 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=-4\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {r}_{2} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=-4\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545861 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545861 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121827" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545861 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545861 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ca/caabcee019c7e97355b6a30f820872207e3f43245d97d3d09a44bf400628a975.png)\n\nFind the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves $r=3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above. Round your answers to $1$ decimal place where appropriate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545861 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cdda3bdc-973b-5424-bda2-1798eb90cfd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545861 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}≈{191.5}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}≈348.5,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place. \n\nNow, we use the equation $r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{1}=4+3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=\\frac{17}{5}=3.4 \\\\ {r}_{2} & =4+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{2}=4+3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=\\frac{17}{5}=3.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )≈(3.4,{191.5}^{∘})\\,$ and $\\,(r,\\theta )≈(3.4,{348.5}^{∘}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545870 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545870 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545870 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324164" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545870 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545870 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/51/515266ff58d998d660634860a07f26e65e0a2791a19a87e1a0f0cd8b8839c9b0.png)\n\nThe circles $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545870 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eaf14619-5650-5c49-bb13-301ef3e6d48c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545870 #edb/ref 1074 "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$$\n(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545872 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545872 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545872 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545872 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3c/3cd5ba688d516f05dbb9c8aa43626ec3194c384034ab612084da5dcc67a14ce6.png)\n\nThe circles $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545872 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed4d6b98-693b-555c-b503-e7cd998ff8be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545872 #edb/ref 1074 "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection.\n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta ={60}^{∘}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{3}{2},{60}^{∘}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
